  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Scattered damaging winds possible this afternoon\nand evening\n\nConvection has gradually increased in intensity crossing the\nsouth central Alleghenies early this afternoon along/just ahead\nof a surface cold front. Downstream, stronger heating has been\nongoing where low stratus have eroded from earlier this \nmorning. Temperatures have warmed into the 70s where this \nstronger heating has occurred, and weak destabilization is\nsupporting expanding coverage of storms. While instability and \nlapse rates are expected to remain rather mild resulting in a\nlow-topped , strong deep- layer flow will support transient \norganized bowing structures, and strong gusts at the surface \nlargely in the absence of significant lightning. The main \ndamaging wind threat appears focus along and to the southeast of\nKJST/KUNV/KIPT. A few line- embedded mesocirculations could \nalso occur, which could yield a brief tornado.\n\n-----------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Blustery and much turning much colder tonight and\nSaturday\n\nThe post-frontal winds gusting 25-35 mph direct much colder air\ninto CPA through tonight into Saturday. Clouds and winds have\nresulted in slightly warmer overnight low temps so advective\nfreeze is probably off the table for the NW mtns. Low level flow\nturns more northerly overnight which should limit lake effect\nresponse, although a few rain/snow showers remain possible over\nthe higher terrain. Min wind chills by Saturday morning will be\nin the 25-35F range across the western and central Alleghenies.\nHighs on Saturday will be 20 to 30 degrees colder vs. Friday. \n\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Frost and freeze conditions Saturday night into\nearly Sunday\n\nFrost/freeze still looks good for Saturday night into early\nSunday morning as clouds fade and winds subside. Issued Freeze\nwatch for active zones in the late innings of the growing\nseason.",
